# Source Control (core)

Apply when creating branches, committing, or opening pull requests.

- **Branch from `main`**; never commit directly to protected branches (`main`).
- **Create the branch if it does not exist yet; do not work on `main` because
  nobody made one.** When a task comes with a tracking ID, the branch is part of
  the task. Check for it before the first edit, not after the work is done:

  ```bash
  git branch --list 'ABC-1234*'            # local
  git ls-remote --heads origin 'ABC-1234*' # remote: may exist with no local copy
  ```

  Three cases, and the middle one is the one that goes wrong:
  - **Exists locally** → `git switch ABC-1234`.
  - **Exists on the remote only** → `git switch ABC-1234` (git tracks it
    automatically). Never `git switch -c` over it: that creates a second,
    unrelated branch from your current HEAD that then fails to push, or worse,
    pushes a divergent history under the same name.
  - **Does not exist** → create it from an up-to-date `main`:
    ```bash
    git switch main && git pull --ff-only
    git switch -c ABC-1234
    ```
- Follow the project's branch/commit naming convention — where a tracking ID
  exists, prefix the commit with it (e.g. `ABC-1234: <summary>`), matching
  recent history. **A branch name is not a commit subject**: the ticket ID alone
  (`ABC-1234`), or ID plus a kebab-case slug (`ABC-1234-add-login-retry`).
  Colons and spaces from the commit form are not usable in a ref name. Match
  what `git branch -a` already shows for that project rather than inventing a
  format.
- Run validation before committing: lint/compile touched files, and run the
  affected tests locally where feasible.
- Keep a clean, focused history — minimal, scoped diffs; no unrelated refactors
  bundled in.
- Do **not** commit secrets, environment-specific config, generated reports, or
  local build artifacts (they belong in `.gitignore`).
- **`SCRATCHPAD.md` at the repo root is a local working log**, not a deliverable —
  it holds the per-chat session summaries (what changed, why, how it was
  verified) written at the end of a working session. It is never committed:
  `templates/common/gitignore-*.txt` ship it under *Local working notes*, so a
  scaffolded repo ignores it from day one; an adopted repo needs the line added
  by hand. Confirm `git check-ignore SCRATCHPAD.md` before any broad `git add`.
- **Commit and push to the PR after each phase** (owner rule, Nov-09-2026; this replaces the
  older "only commit when asked"). A phase is a plan step, a feature slice, a passing test round,
  a fix, or a review round's changes. At the end of each: run the checks, commit, push to the
  branch (`git push -u origin <branch>` the first time), and open the PR on the first push if
  none exists, so the PR shows the work phase by phase. Never push to `main`, never force-push
  without asking, and leave the merge until the owner asks for it.
- **Docs move with every merge, version bump and release build.** In the same change, update
  the README (features, usage, current version, artifact name), any other doc the change
  touches (CHANGELOG, DISCLAIMER, `docs/`), the repo's `SCRATCHPAD.md` entry and the repo's
  Claude memory. A version bump or a release artifact whose README still describes the previous
  version is not done. (Owner rule, Oct-30-2026: mforensics-seeder went from 0.2.1 to 0.2.7 in
  one session while its README still described 0.2.0.)
- **When the owner says "merge", check the docs first** (owner rule, Nov-09-2026). Before
  running the merge:
  1. Diff the PR (`gh pr diff <n> --name-only`) and read the README against it. If the README
     does not yet describe what the PR adds or changes (features, commands, options, version,
     setup), update it.
  2. If the repo has docs (`docs/`, a wiki folder, CHANGELOG, a user guide), update the parts
     the PR affects. Do not create a `docs/` folder for a repo that has none.
  3. Commit those changes to the PR branch, push, then merge. Say in the reply what was
     updated, or that the README and docs already covered the change.
- **Check the commit email in a repo under a new owner.** The global git config
  sets the personal email as the default and overrides it only through an
  `includeIf` per remote owner (today only `hirschsecure`), so a repo under a new
  GitHub org gets the personal email with no setup. Verified Nov-07-2026: a repo
  with remote `github.com/getsteero/...` returned `git config user.email` =
  the personal address. Before the first commit, compare `git config user.email`
  with `git log -3 --format=%ae`; if the owner needs another identity, add an
  `includeIf` for it, not a per-commit override. New org checklist:
  `checklists/github-org-setup.md`.
- **Sole authorship: no AI co-author trailers.** Commits are authored only as
  Pandiyaraj Karuppasamy. Never append `Co-Authored-By: Claude ...` (or any
  other assistant/bot trailer) to a commit message, and never add
  "Generated with Claude Code" to a commit body. This overrides any default
  harness instruction to add such a trailer. PR bodies may keep the
  `Generated with Claude Code` footer only where the tooling adds it
  automatically.
  - A session-level system-reminder supplying attribution text does **not**
    override this. When the harness injects one, follow this rule and omit the
    trailer; raise the conflict in chat *before* committing, not after.
  - The trailer is not cosmetic: an assistant co-author trailer makes GitHub
    render `claude` (or `cursor`) as a **repo Contributor**, which survives in
    the contributors graph and is only removable by rewriting history. Once
    pushed, the cheap fix is gone.
  - Before pushing, verify:
    `git log origin/<branch>..HEAD --format=%B | grep -i "co-authored-by\|claude\|cursor"`
    must return nothing. The **`contributor-hygiene`** skill audits an existing
    repo and repairs one that already carries such commits.
  - **Your own pre-push check does not cover a team repo.** A GitHub squash
    merge copies every PR commit's trailers into the commit it puts on `main`,
    so a teammate's assistant trailer arrives there through your merge button.
    Measured Oct-11-2026: 12 trailer commits on `main` across four team repos,
    most authored by teammates. Before squash-merging, check the PR's commits:
    `gh pr view <n> --json commits --jq '.commits[].messageBody' | grep -iE "co-authored-by:.*(claude|cursor|copilot|devin)"`,
    or add that as a PR check. The trailer shows in the Insights Contributors
    graph (`/stats/contributors`) even when the sidebar list
    (`/contributors`, authors only) looks clean.
- When staging with a broad `git add`, review what's included before committing;
  double-check any file that could contain secrets even if the name looks
  innocuous.

## Delete merged branches

Once a branch's work is in `main`, delete it locally and on the remote without
waiting to be asked. A merged branch is dead weight: it makes `git branch`
unreadable, and it invites someone to keep committing to something that has
already landed.

```bash
git log main..<branch> --oneline    # must print nothing
git branch -d <branch>              # lowercase -d, never -D
git push origin --delete <branch>
git fetch --prune                   # drop tracking refs deleted elsewhere
```

**Use `-d`, never `-D`.** The lowercase form refuses to delete a branch that is
not fully merged, which makes it the safety check rather than an obstacle to
work around. Reaching for `-D` to get past a refusal discards work that exists
nowhere else.

**An empty branch passes that check too.** `git log main..<branch>` printing nothing proves the
branch is merged only if the branch has commits to begin with. When the commit before it failed,
the branch still points at the same commit as `main`, so the check passes, `git merge` answers
"Already up to date" with exit `0`, and `git branch -d` deletes it without complaint. Reproduced
Oct-19-2026 (PowerShell, `git commit -F -` given the message as an argument, so it failed with
`pathspec ... did not match`):

```text
merge exit: 0
git log main..feature --oneline   ->  (nothing)
git branch -d feature             ->  Deleted branch feature (was 1bfbc81)
git rev-list --count main         ->  1        (the feature commit never existed)
git diff --cached --name-only     ->  b.txt    (the work survived only because it was still staged)
```

Prove the commit exists before merging, and treat "Already up to date" from a merge you expected
to do something as a failed commit, not as success:

```bash
git rev-list --count main..<branch>   # must be 1 or more
git diff --cached --quiet             # must be clean: nothing left staged after the commit
```

**Two kinds of branch are never deleted:**

- Anything `git branch --no-merged main` still lists. Unmerged work is somebody's
  unfinished thinking, however stale the branch looks.
- Anything marked `+` in `git branch` output: it is checked out in another
  worktree, which `git worktree list` names. Cursor keeps worktrees under
  `~/.cursor/worktrees/`, and deleting those branches or their worktrees breaks
  a running editor session.

A branch that was merged through a squash or rebase can still show commits in
`git log main..<branch>`, because the SHAs differ even though the content
landed. Confirm with `git cherry -v main <branch>` (every line starting `-` is
already upstream) before deleting, rather than forcing it.

### Renaming a pull request's branch closes the pull request

GitHub ties a pull request to its head branch **by name**. Renaming that branch
(`POST /repos/{o}/{r}/branches/{branch}/rename`, or the web UI) does not move the PR:
GitHub treats the old branch as deleted and closes it. Only PRs whose *base* branch is
renamed get retargeted. Seen Nov-02-2026 on a Dependabot PR that had been carrying a
feature: renaming its branch to the ticket key recorded `closed` and `head_ref_deleted`
on the PR in the same second (`gh api repos/{o}/{r}/issues/{n}/events`).

- **Before renaming any branch**, check `gh pr list --head <branch> --state open`. If a
  PR uses it, don't rename: put the ticket key in the PR title, or open a new PR from a
  ticket-named branch and close the old one with a pointer.
- **To undo it**, recreate the old ref at the same commit and reopen:
  `gh api -X POST repos/{o}/{r}/git/refs -f ref=refs/heads/<old> -f sha=<sha>`, then
  `gh api -X PATCH repos/{o}/{r}/pulls/{n} -f state=open`. Reviews and comments come
  back with it. Delete the renamed copy afterwards so pushes don't split between two
  branches at the same commit.

## Committing an executable script from Windows

Git tracks a file's executable bit, and a Windows working tree has no such bit to read, so a
script committed there lands in the index as **`100644`**. Nothing local notices. The first
Linux consumer — a CI runner, a container, a colleague — fails at the shell:

```
/home/runner/work/_temp/....sh: line 1: ./gradlew: Permission denied
##[error]Process completed with exit code 126
```

Exit **126** is the signature: the file was found and is not executable. It reads like a broken
build script, so the instinct is to debug the script; the script is fine.

Set the bit in the index — not with a local `chmod`, which a Windows checkout cannot represent:

```bash
git update-index --chmod=+x gradlew
git ls-files -s gradlew          # expect 100755, not 100644
```

Applies to every committed wrapper or entrypoint: `gradlew`, `mvnw`, `*.sh`, container
entrypoints, git hooks. Check it whenever a repo starts on Windows and is later built anywhere
else, and add the mode check to review — a `100644` on a shell script is a defect the diff does
not show.

## Pin line endings for anything a non-Windows host executes

The same script has a second Windows-only failure mode. With `core.autocrlf` doing its default
job, a shell script checked out on Windows and re-committed gets CRLF endings, and its shebang
line becomes `#!/bin/sh\r`. Linux then looks for an interpreter literally named `sh\r` and
reports `bad interpreter: No such file or directory` — again nothing to do with the script.

Commit a `.gitattributes` at the repo root, on day one, before the first script lands:

```gitattributes
# Normalise, so a Windows checkout does not rewrite every file.
* text=auto eol=lf

# Executed by /bin/sh on Linux. CRLF here breaks the shebang, so this must stay LF.
gradlew text eol=lf
*.sh    text eol=lf

# Windows-only scripts keep CRLF so they open correctly in Notepad and cmd.
*.bat text eol=crlf
*.ps1 text eol=crlf

# Binary: never touch, never diff.
*.jar binary
*.png binary
*.keystore binary
*.jks binary
```

Verify what is actually stored, rather than what the working tree shows — the two differ by
design here:

```bash
git show origin/main:gradlew | head -1 | od -c   # expect ...  h  
 , never  
  

```

Without `.gitattributes` this is invisible until it is someone else's CI failure: `git status`
is clean, the file runs fine on Windows, and only a Linux execution surfaces it.

### `git archive` applies the same conversion

A deploy bundle exported from a Windows checkout is not "the committed bytes".
`git archive` runs the checkout filters, so with `core.autocrlf=true` every text file
without an explicit `eol` attribute comes out CRLF, even though the index holds LF.
Reproduced (one LF file, same commit):

```bash
git -c core.autocrlf=true  archive HEAD | tar -xO f.py | od -c   # a \r \n b \r \n
git -c core.autocrlf=false archive HEAD | tar -xO f.py | od -c   # a \n b \n
```

Seen Oct-09-2026 exporting a Python service for a Linux VM: 78 of 81 files arrived CRLF
while `git ls-files --eol` showed `i/lf w/crlf attr/`. A sibling repo with `* text eol=lf`
in `.gitattributes` exported clean, which is the fix above doing its job. Where you cannot
change the repo, export with `git -c core.autocrlf=false archive <ref>` and check the
result by reading bytes in code before shipping it. Under Git Bash, `grep -rlI $'\r'`
reported 91 CRLF files in a bundle that had none.

**A deploy tool that wraps `git archive` must pass `-c core.autocrlf=false` itself.** The
person running it will not remember, and the repo may have no `.gitattributes`. Seen
Nov-09-2026: a home-grown deploy tool archived a repo with only image builds in mind. The
built images were fine, but a shell script bind-mounted into a Postgres container
(`/docker-entrypoint-initdb.d/001-app-role.sh`) arrived CRLF, failed with
`/bin/sh^M: bad interpreter`, and left the new database without its app role while the
container reported healthy (see
[deploy-safety](../web-app/deploy-safety.mdc#moving-a-containerized-postgres-to-another-host)).
Add a test that commits an LF `.sh` in a repo with `core.autocrlf=true`, runs the tool's
packaging step and asserts the bundle has no `\r\n`; it fails without the flag.

### A pattern with a slash only matches from its own folder

`.gitattributes` patterns follow `.gitignore` rules: a pattern that contains a `/` is
matched relative to the directory holding that `.gitattributes`, not anywhere below it.
A rule written for one copy of a folder silently misses a second copy nested deeper,
and that copy gets line-ending conversion on the next Windows checkout. Reproduced, with
the attributes file in `kit/`:

```text
hotpatch/files/** -text      ->  kit/hotpatch/files/a.py: text: unset
                                 kit/share/hotpatch/files/a.py: text: unspecified
**/hotpatch/files/** -text   ->  both: text: unset
```

Seen Oct-12-2026: a hot-patch bundle, verified by sha256 on the target host, had a second
copy in a hand-off folder, and only the first was protected. Prefix with `**/` when the
same folder can appear at more than one depth, and check the paths that matter rather
than trusting the pattern:

```bash
git check-attr text eol -- <path> <the other copy>
```

## Other sessions share the working tree

Several sessions (Claude Code, Cursor, a person) often work in the same checkout
at once. They leave uncommitted files in the tree, push their own commits to
main, and **keep editing those files after pushing**. So their local copy can be
newer than what is on main, and a conflict between "your" commit and main may
really be between their newer local work and their older pushed work.

What happened (devdash, Oct-10-2026): a Workflow Cleanup feature landed on main
from another session while a later change of theirs (a `--start` bound across
two scripts, the module, the page, the tests and one route in `main.py`) existed
only in the working tree. My commit of the tree conflicted with main. The
obvious fix, drop the duplicate commit, would have deleted their newer work.

- **Back up before any history surgery:** `git branch backup/<branch>-before-<op>`
  before a reset, a rebase, a dropped commit or a conflict resolution. Never
  `git reset --hard` or `git checkout -- <file>` over changes this session did
  not make.
- **Diff their files against `origin/main`, not just yours,** and include the
  shared files where both features touch (route modules, menu config,
  `.env.example`):
  `git diff origin/main backup/<branch> -- <their files>`. Local can be the
  newer side.
- **Carry their newer edits forward as a commit of their own.** Neither side
  wins wholesale. In add/add conflicts inside shared files, keep both blocks.
- **Prove nothing was lost:** `git diff backup/<branch> HEAD --stat` must show
  only ordering differences in the shared files. A sort-and-compare of lines
  gives false alarms across CRLF and LF, so read the real diff.
- **Commit only this session's work** unless asked. Ask before committing files
  another session wrote.

### Splitting two features interleaved inside one file

`git add -p` is interactive, so an agent cannot use it. When two features'
hunks sit next to each other in the same file, stage a version of the file that
contains only one feature, without touching the working tree:

```bash
# build the one-feature version of the file however suits (a script that drops
# the other feature's blocks), then put that content in the index:
blob=$(git hash-object -w --stdin < /tmp/main.only-feature-a.py)
git update-index --cacheinfo 100644,"$blob",app/main.py
git diff --cached app/main.py      # check what will be committed
```

Then check the staged snapshot on its own. `git checkout-index -a --prefix=/tmp/staged/`
writes the index to a separate directory, where the suite can run without the
other feature's untracked files. Commit, and stage the remaining feature
normally.

## Verify the remote, not the working tree

`.gitignore` describes intent; the remote holds the truth, and the two diverge whenever a file
was tracked before the ignore rule existed — ignore rules do not apply retroactively to tracked
files. After the first push of a repo, audit what actually shipped:

```bash
git ls-tree -r --name-only origin/main | grep -iE '/build/|local\.properties|\.jks$|\.keystore|settings\.local'
```

Do this for credentials and machine-specific config especially. A file holding an absolute SDK
path or a signing key is not obviously secret by name, and `git add -A` does not ask.

### The reverse: a broad ignore rule hides source you meant to commit

A template ignore such as `data/` matches a directory of that name at **any depth**, so a Python
package called `pages/data/` is silently never added: `git add -A` skips it without a word. On the
author's machine everything passes, because the files exist on disk. Measured Oct-18-2026 on a
test suite merged with five page-object files missing: `git check-ignore -v
tests/.../pages/data/grid_page.py` printed `.gitignore:37:data/`, and the first fresh clone (a Linux
run) died at collection with `ModuleNotFoundError: No module named '...pages.data'`.

Check for it before merging anything with new directories, and re-include what is source:

```bash
git ls-files --others --ignored --exclude-standard | grep -v __pycache__   # ignored files that exist
git clone -q . /tmp/clonecheck && ls /tmp/clonecheck/<new-dir>              # what a collaborator gets
```

```gitignore
data/
!tests/integration/controllab/pages/data/
```

The same applies to `dl/`, `downloads/`, `build/`, `out/`, `tmp/` and `lib/` in the hub's ignore
templates. A suite that only ever ran in the working tree has not been tested.

### Untracking a file deletes it from every other checkout

The fix for a committed config file is `git rm --cached <file>` plus an ignore rule. The file
stays on *your* disk. But any checkout of a branch that still tracks it re-adopts the file, and
when the untrack commit is merged or pulled there, git **deletes it from disk**. Every
collaborator who pulls loses their local `config.ini`, and you lose yours the moment you switch
back to `main` and fast-forward. Reproduced Oct-18-2026 in a scratch repo:

```text
git switch -c fix; git rm --cached config.ini; commit   -> config.ini still on disk
git switch main                                         -> still on disk, tracked again
git merge --ff-only fix                                 -> config.ini deleted
```

It also happens during a history rewrite (`filter-branch` checks the rewritten tip out). So:

- Copy the real file aside before switching, merging or rewriting, and restore it after.
- Ship a committed template (`example_config.ini`) and, for an installed tool, a command that
  writes it (`--init-config`), so a deleted copy is cheap to recreate.
- Say in the PR body that pulling it deletes the local file, so collaborators copy theirs first.

## Reviewing a PR and posting comments
To review the current branch's PR and publish the findings as inline comments
(authored as you, via the `gh` CLI), use the
[pr-review-comment](../../skills/pr-review-comment/SKILL.md) skill. For the
underlying GitHub comment API mechanics, see
[github-pr-comments](../../skills/github-pr-comments/SKILL.md).

To measure commits, lines, PRs, branches or reviews per person from GitHub
(dashboards, reports), see
[github-contribution-metrics](../../skills/github-contribution-metrics/SKILL.md):
squash merges double-count, deleted branches survive only as PR heads, and raw
review submissions overstate reviewing many times over.
